  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nTemperatures today will generally be in the 40s and 50s this \nmorning before warming into the 80s to near 90 degrees this \nafternoon. With this, dry conditions will prevail for most with \nonly some passing clouds, though cannot rule out a sprinkle at \ntimes. Low temperatures tonight will be a bit warmer in the upper \n40s and 50s. \n\nHot temperatures return Thursday as the cyclonic flow that \nbrought the cooler temperatures to the area the past few days is \nreplaced by a building ridge of high pressure aloft. Current \nforecast high temperatures are in the upper 80s to 90s under \nmostly sunny skies. Later in the day, a backdoor cold front looks \nto move through, bringing breezy northwesterly winds to the area. \nThe combination of these winds, hot temperatures, and very dry \nhumidity values will lead to elevated fire weather conditions \nacross much of the area, though wind gusts limited to the teens \nand 20s mph should help limit the high end of this risk.\n\nFriday looks to be another hot day, though forecast high \ntemperatures have decreased a few degrees now that models are in a\nbit better agreement regarding the Thursday backdoor cold front \npassage. Current forecast high temperatures are in the 90s for \nmost, with only a few 100 degree readings possible over the plains\n(medium chance). Dry conditions will continue. \n\nSaturday into Sunday have now trended a bit warmer with high \ntemperatures in the upper 80s to lower 100s, warmest on Saturday. \nWhile mostly dry conditions look to continue, the upper level flow\npattern begins to get a bit more messy during this time. With \nthis, there is a low chance (10 percent) of seeing precipitation \nwith any shortwave trough that progresses through the area. Along \nwith the low chance of precipitation, breezy conditions will \nexist. Therefore, the combination of wind, hot temperatures, and \nlow relative humidity values will lead to elevated fire weather \nconditions at times. \n\nMonday into next week, a more active weather pattern with higher \natmospheric moisture looks to develop, though models have backed \noff a bit on the strength of this pattern change. With that said, \nthe chance of precipitation does begin to increase Monday into \nTuesday. LA \n\nSmoke will continue to create hazy skies at times through the\nweek. As the ridge begins to break down and flatten, turning upper\nlevel flow out of the west, more smoke may move into the region\nand affect air quality and visibility. For the latest air quality,\nvisit airnow.gov. Archer",
